WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.170
I have a ton of non-Trump news to discuss
with you today, and in a moment, we're going

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.170
to be focusing in on Arizona and the Senate
race that's going on in Arizona.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:12.470
But before I do that, I want to talk about
the Senate more broadly a little bit.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.889
I don't know with everything that's going
on.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:20.820
How many people in my audience are following
what's going on with the Senate for November

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.340
of 2020.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:27.040
But it is increasingly clear that if we all
come together, if we do the hard work, if

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.160
we do the phone banking, if we get out there
and vote, there is a realistic shot that Republicans

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.350
could lose control of the Senate in November.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:40.940
Now, a year ago, when we were talking about
this, I was mostly saying it's like a 5 percent

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:41.940
chance.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.640
I mean, Democrats could take the Senate, but
really we're we're probably going to have

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.670
to wait until 2022 to see Republicans lose
control of the Senate.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.040
And then six months ago, things that shifted
a little bit.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.700
I did a segment pointing out it's in play.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.030
It's probably still like a 20 percent shot
at taking the Senate for Democrats.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:05.969
There is now an increasingly realistic scenario
where Democrats snatch control of the Senate

00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:07.590
from Republicans in November.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:14.899
Now, to refresh the numbers, right now, the
balance of power is 53 to 47 in favor of Republicans.

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:22.350
That is the 47 is 45 Democrats and then two
independents, Bernie Sanders and Angus King.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.539
That's how you get to 47.

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:30.810
If the Senate is tied 50/50, the vise president
gets to cast the tie breaking vote.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:39.240
So right now, if Democrats want control, they
need a net gain of four seats or three seats

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.020
plus the presidency, because then Joe Biden's
vise president becomes the tiebreaker in a

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.020
50 50 Senate.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.310
Now Democrats are going to lose Alabama.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.329
This is just a reality, Alabama.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:52.360
There's this Senate seat that used to be Jeff
Sessions seat.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.779
Republican Jeff Sessions became Trump's beleaguered
attorney general.

00:01:56.779 --> 00:02:02.179
Doug Jones defeated serial accused sex assaulter
Roy Moore.

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:06.219
But that is an anomaly, an anomaly, depending
on how you like to pronounce it.

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:08.190
And Jeff Sessions is running for that seat
again.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:09.549
And he's almost certain to win.

00:02:09.549 --> 00:02:10.549
He's winning by a ton.

00:02:10.549 --> 00:02:13.210
So in practice, losing that one.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:20.599
This means Democrats need five wins against
Republicans or four wins plus the presidency.

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:25.329
And it is not a crazy scenario at this point
to imagine that that might happen.

00:02:25.329 --> 00:02:30.420
It would be a major turn of events from what
we would have been looking at six months ago

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.420
in great part.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.920
You would blame Trump, you could blame the
virus.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.659
But Republicans have a very difficult path
now to defending control of the Senate compared

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:42.379
to six or twelve months ago.

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:44.589
You've got 100 senators.

00:02:44.589 --> 00:02:46.490
They serve six year terms.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.560
That means every two years, a third of the
Senate is up for reelection.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.599
Right now, we have 33 races plus two special
elections, Arizona and Georgia.

00:02:56.599 --> 00:03:03.730
And of those, 35 Republicans have to defend
23, whereas Democrats are only defending 12.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:08.709
In other words, Republicans have 23 seats
they could lose, whereas Democrats have only

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:10.370
12 seats that they could lose.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.879
Now, I've already mentioned Alabama and Democrats,
almost certainly Democrats are going to lose

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:16.640
that Alabama Senate seat.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.859
Let's look at the best shots for Democrats
to take seats from Republicans.

00:03:20.859 --> 00:03:25.030
First of all, you've got Colorado where John
Hickenlooper, the Democrat, is leading the

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:28.330
incumbent Republican, Cory Gardner, by 13.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.730
That will very likely flip in Arizona.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:36.999
Mark Kelly is leading Martha McSally, the
incumbent Republican, by an average of nine.

00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:40.870
Now in one recent poll, it's 13 and we'll
talk about that a little later.

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:46.030
In Maine, you have Sarah Gideon, the Democratic
challenger to longtime Republican Senator

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:49.689
Susan Collins, ahead by about two and a half.

00:03:49.689 --> 00:03:52.870
And then in North Carolina, it's very close.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:57.540
Cal Cunningham, the Democratic challenger
ahead of incumbent Republican Senator Thom

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.049
Tillis by one.

00:03:59.049 --> 00:04:00.439
I mean, that's like a tie.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:01.950
It's the margin of error.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:07.980
Then you've got some longer shots for Democrats
in terms of the longer shots in Montana.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.950
Steve Bullock has a very good recent poll
against the incumbent Republican, Steve Daines.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.160
I'm still calling it a longshot because it
is just one poll and it is Montana.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.269
But Montana doesn't look bad for Democrats.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:24.150
Then you've got Georgia, Kansas and Iowa,
which are, as we're calling them.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.910
Those are long shots for Democrats, but they
are not completely impossible.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:32.800
So the news is the Senate is very much in
play.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.730
It's very much worth fighting for.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.720
This has been a big reversal over the last
six.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:43.260
And particularly if you look at what the polling
was 12 months ago, but voters need to vote

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.260
otherwise.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.260
This is nothing.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.070
This is going to be another opportunity lost
if people don't get out and vote.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.639
I don't want to see that happen.

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:57.169
Now, let's focus in on one Senate race, and
I'll use it to look more broadly at the signal

00:04:57.169 --> 00:04:59.450
that the Senate races could.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.129
Send about the presidential race.

00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:02.679
Let's dig into Arizona right now

